<chapter xml:id="ch17-complex-numbers-fourier-ideas-and-the-road-ahead" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Complex Numbers, Fourier Ideas, and the Road Ahead</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>The last chapter ended with a strange-looking promise:</p>

  <md>e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta.</md>

  <p>The formula says that an exponential can trace a circle.  At first that sounds impossible.
Exponential growth moves away from <m>0</m>.  Sine and cosine move back and forth.  A
circle turns.  Why should one formula connect all three?</p>

  <p>Taylor series gave the clue.  The series for <m>e^x</m>, <m>\cos x</m>, and <m>\sin x</m> have the
same factorials hiding inside them.  The missing ingredient is a number whose square is</p>

  <md>-1.</md>

  <p>That number is called <m>i</m>.  It opens a new plane of numbers, and in that plane the
exponential becomes the natural language of rotation.</p>
  </introduction>

